export const BUILTIN_SKILLS:readonly BuiltinSkill[] = JSON.parse("[{\"id\":\"browser-operator\",\"name\":\"Browser Operator\",\"priority\":\"P0\",\"category\":\"browser\",\"description\":\"Operate an authorized browser using DOM snapshots and repeatable actions.\",\"engine\":\"agent-browser + Microsoft Playwright MCP\",\"requires\":[\"agent-browser\"],\"scopes\":[\"browser:read\",\"browser:interact\"],\"upstream\":\"https://github.com/vercel-labs/agent-browser\",\"license\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"optIn\":false,\"content\":\"---\\nname: browser-operator\\ndescription: Operate an authorized browser using DOM snapshots and repeatable actions.\\npriority: P0\\ncategory: browser\\nstatus: builtin-guidance\\n---\\n\\n# Browser Operator\\n\\n**Scope:** agent-browser + Microsoft Playwright MCP. **Source:** https://github.com/vercel-labs/agent-browser\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\nUse existing Remote Arc shared_tab_read for read-only work on a tab the user shared first. In the user's own browser with the Remote Arc Browser extension 0.4.0 or later, shared_tab_open opens a tab on a site the user allowed (SITE_NOT_ALLOWED means ask the user to click Allow once) and shared_tab_act clicks, fills, selects, presses keys, navigates and waits there; final submits need effect=submit with an idempotency_key and are settled with action=confirm. For multi-step work on a linked computer, use the Remote Arc Browser Runner (browser_runner_*) when it is enabled for the device: a separate Runner window by default, or the user's own Chrome with browser=your_chrome when the user asks for it; otherwise use locally approved agent-browser or Playwright, in a separate profile; never attach to personal sessions without explicit permission. Steps: 1) confirm device, origin and requested interaction scope; 2) snapshot DOM and locate stable accessible selectors; 3) plan bounded clicks/fills; 4) ask permission for modifying site state, checkout, account settings, irreversible submissions; 5) verify changed page and report evidence. Keep navigation URLs within allowed origins. Never use anti-bot evasion, cookie export, credential harvesting or silent downloads. Headless browser must be explicitly chosen; a Chrome extension tab is a different permission boundary. CLI examples only after installation: agent-browser open https://example.com; agent-browser snapshot -i; agent-browser close. If browser CLI is absent, use existing Remote Arc browser tools and report limits.\\n\\n## Device and permission gates\\n\\nRequired authorization scopes: browser:read, browser:interact. Scripts, OS/desktop actions, external service side effects, media generation and paid API usage require independent user-approved execution permissions. This skill does not elevate Remote Arc Tool privileges or authorize use of hidden credentials.\\n\\n## Preflight\\n\\nCheck the engines this skill names before relying on them, with tools you already have (for example `<engine> --version` through start_process, or list_directory on its install folder). Missing executors must be installed explicitly on the correct user device. A Skill being built-in is NOT evidence an engine is present.\\n\\n## Evidence of completion\\n\\nReport input constraints, executed tools and device, verified output path/result, permissions used, cost (if known) and failures. Do not imply successful execution from merely writing a plan.\\n\"},{\"id\":\"autonomous-browser\",\"name\":\"Autonomous Browser\",\"priority\":\"P0\",\"category\":\"browser\",\"description\":\"Plan multi-step browser work using browser-use with an approved model budget.\",\"engine\":\"browser-use Python library\",\"requires\":[\"browser-use\"],\"scopes\":[\"browser:read\",\"browser:interact\"],\"upstream\":\"https://github.com/browser-use/browser-use\",\"license\":\"MIT\",\"optIn\":true,\"content\":\"---\\nname: autonomous-browser\\ndescription: Plan multi-step browser work using browser-use with an approved model budget.\\npriority: P0\\ncategory: browser\\nstatus: builtin-guidance\\n---\\n\\n# Autonomous Browser\\n\\n**Scope:** browser-use Python library. **Source:** https://github.com/browser-use/browser-use\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\nUse only when deterministic DOM actions fail or multi-step planning is essential. browser-use is a separate Python reasoning engine and may use chargeable LLM APIs. Do not create paid cloud sessions by default. Require an explicit device/browser permission, named objective, URL allowlist, max iterations, timeout, model budget and human checkpoints. Prefer local browser and a caller-provided model account; never assume API credits are free. Inspect planned actions, execute only authorized ones, and save a scrubbed action/evidence log. Stop on CAPTCHA, account lock, MFA, payments, new unknown domains and repeated failures. A user can instead choose the zero-model-cost Browser Operator. No autostart and no implicit installation.\\n\\n## Device and permission gates\\n\\nRequired authorization scopes: browser:read, browser:interact. Scripts, OS/desktop actions, external service side effects, media generation and paid API usage require independent user-approved execution permissions. This skill does not elevate Remote Arc Tool privileges or authorize use of hidden credentials.\\n\\n## Preflight\\n\\nCheck the engines this skill names before relying on them, with tools you already have (for example `<engine> --version` through start_process, or list_directory on its install folder). Missing executors must be installed explicitly on the correct user device. A Skill being built-in is NOT evidence an engine is present.\\n\\n## Evidence of completion\\n\\nReport input constraints, executed tools and device, verified output path/result, permissions used, cost (if known) and failures. Do not imply successful execution from merely writing a plan.\\n\"},{\"id\":\"desktop-operator\",\"name\":\"Desktop Operator\",\"priority\":\"P0\",\"category\":\"desktop\",\"description\":\"Move pointer, drag, type and click in a permitted local desktop session.\",\"engine\":\"PyAutoGUI / native OS accessibility\",\"requires\":[\"python3\"],\"scopes\":[\"computer:read\",\"computer:write\"],\"upstream\":\"https://github.com/asweigart/pyautogui\",\"license\":\"BSD-3-Clause\",\"optIn\":true,\"content\":\"---\\nname: desktop-operator\\ndescription: Move pointer, drag, type and click in a permitted local desktop session.\\npriority: P0\\ncategory: desktop\\nstatus: builtin-guidance\\n---\\n\\n# Desktop Operator\\n\\n**Scope:** PyAutoGUI / native OS accessibility. **Source:** https://github.com/asweigart/pyautogui\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\nPrefer native accessibility tree/API when available, then screenshot-based coordinates as fallback. On macOS request Accessibility + Screen Recording permission through the OS; on Windows control the actual Windows desktop via an explicit native helper, not a WSL X display. Use existing Remote Arc process permission and scoped device approval for scripts. For mouse movement, calculate a short monotonic path with easing, bounded duration and screen dimensions; screenshot before/after to verify. Never silently move the pointer on a device the user is actively using. Confirm focused app and coordinates before typing, especially passwords, financial transfers or destructive actions. Do not attempt to bypass OS consent dialogs. PyAutoGUI installation and permission are opt-in per device.\\n\\n## Device and permission gates\\n\\nRequired authorization scopes: computer:read, computer:write. Scripts, OS/desktop actions, external service side effects, media generation and paid API usage require independent user-approved execution permissions. This skill does not elevate Remote Arc Tool privileges or authorize use of hidden credentials.\\n\\n## Preflight\\n\\nCheck the engines this skill names before relying on them, with tools you already have (for example `<engine> --version` through start_process, or list_directory on its install folder). Missing executors must be installed explicitly on the correct user device. A Skill being built-in is NOT evidence an engine is present.\\n\\n## Evidence of completion\\n\\nReport input constraints, executed tools and device, verified output path/result, permissions used, cost (if known) and failures. Do not imply successful execution from merely writing a plan.\\n\"},{\"id\":\"video-creator\",\"name\":\"Video Creator\",\"priority\":\"P0\",\"category\":\"media\",\"description\":\"Choose Remotion, HyperFrames or OpenFilm for finished local videos; prepare ordinary dependencies automatically and ask before large Qwen TTS/ACE-Step downloads.\",\"engine\":\"Remotion 4.0.538 / HyperFrames 0.8.193 / OpenFilm 0.2.5 + FFmpeg\",\"requires\":[\"node\",\"ffmpeg\",\"git\"],\"scopes\":[\"computer:read\",\"computer:write\"],\"upstream\":\"https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm\",\"license\":\"MIT; generated media follows each provider's terms\",\"optIn\":false,\"content\":\"---\\nname: video-creator\\ndescription: Create finished local videos with Remotion, OpenFilm or HyperFrames, including animation, maps, narration and music. Automatically prepare ordinary dependencies; ask before large Qwen TTS or ACE-Step installs.\\npriority: P0\\ncategory: media\\nstatus: builtin-guidance\\n---\\n\\n# Video Creator\\n\\nWrite the film and deliver the rendered file. Remotion 4.0.538, OpenFilm 0.2.5 and HyperFrames 0.8.193 render code on the user's computer. Read remotion-video for React scenes and Three.js cameras, hyperframes-video for HTML/SVG/GSAP motion, and openfilm-video for shot assembly. A named engine takes priority over automatic routing. Core rendering, maps and local transcription need no model API key. Device compute, storage, downloads and the calling AI's allowance still apply.\\n\\n## Device and permission gates\\n\\nUse an online user device with read_file, write_file and start_process, and a task folder inside its Trusted Write Locations. The requested media task authorizes preparing the ordinary dependencies below within existing device permissions. Do not ask again merely because Remotion, OpenFilm, HyperFrames, FFmpeg, Chromium, map packages/data or base Whisper are absent. Announce the source, approximate size and destination, then install and continue to the finished artifact. This does not authorize system privileges, unrelated software, cloud rendering, paid APIs, uploads or publication.\\n\\nAll rendering, inference, audio processing and output files stay on the selected user device. Dependency downloads contact npm, official GitHub releases and Hugging Face; never send user recordings or project assets to those services. Keep caches and tools under <workspace>/.ra-media-tools. Respect disk-space failures and OS permission prompts; do not clean unrelated user files to make room.\\n\\n## Prepare and run\\n\\nThis package includes the pinned helper scripts/local-media.mjs.\\n- Arc: prepare_skill {id:\\\"video-creator\\\",deviceId,workspace} saves the helper and returns its directory.\\n- Connect: read_skill {id:\\\"video-creator\\\"} lists the helper SHA-256 and bytes. Read read_skill {id:\\\"video-creator\\\",path:\\\"scripts/local-media.mjs\\\",offset:0}, follow nextOffset until null, write the exact chunks on the device and check their SHA-256 against the manifest. Never reconstruct a helper from a truncated response.\\n- Resource-only clients can read remotearc://skills/video-creator/scripts/local-media.mjs. The skill's mere availability does not mean its engines are installed.\\n\\nUse Node.js 22.2+ with npm. If absent, automatically prepare a portable official Node distribution inside .ra-media-tools/node: choose the device OS/architecture from nodejs.org/dist, verify its archive against that release's SHASUMS256.txt, extract locally and use that node/npm path for this task. Do not require a global package manager or administrator installation. If an OS prerequisite cannot be installed within existing permissions, explain the specific blocker and retain completed work.\\n\\nRun, with absolute paths and quoted arguments:\\n    node \\\"<helper>/scripts/local-media.mjs\\\" ensure --workspace \\\"<workspace>\\\" --components openfilm\\nThis installs only the requested engine, missing portable FFmpeg/ffprobe and Chromium; initial setup is usually hundreds of MB. For Remotion use --components remotion and read remotion-video; render with --engine remotion --entry src/index.tsx --composition <registered ID>. For HyperFrames use --components hyperframes. For a map use --components maps; it caches d3/d3-geo-projection/topojson-client and Natural Earth country geometry. For subtitles read transcribe-subtitles and use --components whisper (base model ~148 MB plus executable/build tools). Reuse the cache and package-lock on later tasks; do not reinstall everything for each job. Check the returned engine CLI paths and version. An ensure result is dependency readiness, never task completion.\\n\\nOpenFilm commands must receive OPENFILM_NO_BROWSER=1 and OPENFILM_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1. The helper does this and confines browser/download caches to the workspace. Never open Studio or preview on the user's screen merely to show progress. For direct CLI invocations, preserve the helper environment (PATH includes .ra-media-tools/bin and Node; PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH points to .ra-media-tools/browsers). Read the installed OpenFilm manual by running its CLI without arguments before authoring; read HyperFrames --help for exact command flags.\\n\\n## Author and deliver the film\\n\\n1. Infer the brief from context; use 30 s, 1920×1080 by default or 1080×1920 for Shorts/Reels. Choose language, palette and source assets. Create a new film folder within the workspace; preserve existing projects.\\n2. Build deterministic pages from the user's footage, photos, logo, authorized screenshots, real data or code graphics. In OpenFilm each page exposes window.film = {duration, frame(t)} and draws solely from t. No timers, random values, transitions or Date-driven animation. Put shared styles/helpers in shared.js.\\n3. In film.html set the viewport width/height, one section per track, upper tracks first, clips with at timestamps, static clips with #t=0,length, and audio clips only in film.html. In HyperFrames use index.html with its documented composition metadata and paused GSAP timelines; bundle fonts/scripts locally.\\n4. Add sound if requested. Reuse already installed native voices (macOS say or Windows speech), supplied recordings/music, or a simple local FFmpeg synth bed. Qwen/ACE installation follows the separate consent gate below. Avoid silent output when narration or music was part of the brief.\\n5. Render a real file:\\n       node \\\"<helper>/scripts/local-media.mjs\\\" render --workspace \\\"<workspace>\\\" --engine openfilm --project \\\"<film folder>\\\" --output \\\"<new output>.mp4\\\"\\n   HyperFrames uses --engine hyperframes. The helper installs missing ordinary tools, runs look/lint, renders, checks positive duration/video tracks, decodes the result and writes a SHA-256 artifact receipt. Run long work with start_process background:true, then inspect process_status/process_output through completion. Never treat a started process as success.\\n6. Inspect contact sheets/keyframes at beginning, middle and end, including title transitions and the longest text. Check legibility, clipping, collisions, blank frames, aspect ratio and audio sync. Read actual ffprobe duration/resolution/size; compare them with the brief. Check audio loudness when audio was requested. Fix observed problems and rerender to a new output.\\n7. Deliver the MP4 itself, its absolute local path, duration, resolution and size, together with editable project sources when useful. Keep the local file as the durable copy. create_file_resource can give a temporary download; state its expiry and regenerate on request. Upload or publish only where the user requested, using supported tools and existing permissions. Do not promise a permanent public link from a temporary bearer URL.\\n\\n## Maps — local and reusable\\n\\nFor a finished static world map:\\n    node \\\"<helper>/scripts/local-media.mjs\\\" map --workspace \\\"<workspace>\\\" --output \\\"<new map>.svg\\\" --highlight NZL,CHN\\nUse ISO three-letter highlights. The helper caches Natural Earth public-domain geometry, pins its source revision and SHA-256 and writes an SVG plus artifact receipt.\\n\\nFor animation, copy the cached GeoJSON and local d3 distributions into the film's assets folder; load those local files in pages. Use d3.geoMercator, geoNaturalEarth1 or a suitable regional projection, and draw regions/camera motion from t. No online tile/API/CDN dependency during rendering. Country geometry is the default; fetch official Natural Earth admin-1, coastline or river datasets locally when the brief needs them, retaining source revision/hash. Do not invent province detail from country data. Check disputed boundaries and labels against the user's intended presentation.\\n\\n## Voice-over, music and maps — local, no keys\\n\\n**Voice-over (Qwen3-TTS, Apache-2.0).** Before installing Qwen TTS or fetching missing weights, **ask the user for explicit approval**: disclose multi-GB downloads (PyTorch plus weights), estimated expanded disk use, chosen model, destination and CPU/GPU expectations. Do not download Qwen while awaiting approval; continue independent visuals/subtitles or use an existing native voice if suitable. Requesting narration is not installation consent. An already installed compatible model or existing explicit consent within the same scope can be reused without asking again.\\n\\nAfter approval, use a dedicated local venv and model cache under .ra-media-tools/qwen. Select PyTorch wheels for the actual device: CUDA only with supported NVIDIA hardware, MPS only where verified, CPU otherwise. Install qwen-tts and huggingface_hub with pinned versions recorded in a receipt; download Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-0.6B-CustomVoice and its tokenizer into that cache. Qwen3TTSModel.from_pretrained with the verified backend, generate_custom_voice(text, language=\\\"Chinese\\\", speaker=\\\"Vivian\\\"), then save the returned waveform with its actual sample rate. Check duration, intelligibility and clipping before adding it to the film. No cloud TTS fallback without authorization.\\n\\n**Music bed (ACE-Step 1.5, MIT).** **Ask for explicit approval** before cloning/installing dependencies or downloading weights: disclose 10 GB+ downloads, expanded disk use, destination and hardware/runtime estimates. A generic music request does not authorize installation. Reuse an installed compatible model or existing scoped approval. While approval is pending, continue independent work or use the supplied/local synth bed if suitable.\\n\\nAfter approval, clone https://github.com/ACE-Step/ACE-Step-1.5 at a recorded revision under .ra-media-tools/acestep, use its isolated environment and actual supported device backend, and record installed versions/model hashes. NVIDIA examples are not a promise of CPU/Intel Mac performance. If its local API is needed, start acestep-api --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8001; check that installed version's schema, submit a bounded duration, wait for completion and copy the audio into film assets. Never start a public listener. Inspect the generated audio before delivery.\\n\\n**Maps & data.** Automatically prepare only the packages and datasets needed by the brief as described above. No extra installation question for ordinary local map preparation.\\n\\n## Evidence of completion\\n\\nReport the device, rendered file and editable project paths, verified duration/resolution/size, selected engines and remaining limitations. The helper's artifact receipt proves file/decoder checks; visual quality and speech accuracy still need review. Do not claim delivery from a plan, dependency install, sample scaffold or failed render. Paid providers, image models, Remotion licensing and publication retain their separate authorization requirements.\\n\"},{\"id\":\"image-studio\",\"name\":\"Image Studio\",\"priority\":\"P1\",\"category\":\"media\",\"description\":\"Draw posters, illustrations and seeded generative art with local code; optionally synthesize, retouch and upscale through a user-hosted ComfyUI backend.\",\"engine\":\"Canvas/Python + p5.js; optional ComfyUI\",\"requires\":[\"node or python3\"],\"scopes\":[\"computer:read\",\"computer:write\"],\"upstream\":\"https://github.com/Comfy-Org/ComfyUI\",\"license\":\"GPL-3.0; weights separate\",\"optIn\":true,\"content\":\"---\\nname: image-studio\\ndescription: Draw original images with local Canvas/Python or seeded p5.js; use a user-hosted ComfyUI backend for photographic synthesis, retouching and upscaling.\\npriority: P1\\ncategory: media\\nstatus: builtin-guidance\\n---\\n\\n# Image Studio\\n\\n**Scope:** local code graphics and optional local ComfyUI.\\n**Community sources:** Canvas Design and Algorithmic Art by Anthropic, available as `community-canvas-design` and `community-algorithmic-art` in the Remote Arc Skills catalog.\\n**Model backend:** https://github.com/Comfy-Org/ComfyUI\\n\\n## Choose the output workflow\\n\\nFor posters, covers, diagrams, editorial graphics, abstract art or generative patterns, use code graphics first: Canvas/Python for composed static work, or the packaged Algorithmic Art/p5.js workflow for seeded, interactive systems. These do not require an image model, GPU model download or paid image API. Read the selected skill and its resources, then create the actual local artifact.\\n\\nFor photographic synthesis, editing supplied photographs or model-based upscaling, check the user's local ComfyUI endpoint, compatible accelerator and existing model weights first. ComfyUI and weights are not bundled. Ask before downloading model weights or using a paid provider; there is no default GPU/cloud/API spending. Keep the endpoint on loopback and never expose it publicly without authentication. If no suitable backend exists, explain the missing dependency and do not claim a photograph was generated.\\n\\n## Local code graphics\\n\\n1. Confirm size, intended use, required text and whether the user wants a static image or an interactive generator.\\n2. Read Canvas Design or Algorithmic Art and the relevant resources. Materialize needed text resources in the authorized project folder. Use a local renderer; automatically prepare ordinary lightweight libraries within existing authorization.\\n3. Use local fonts. Canvas Design includes font license texts and a pinned font manifest, with optional binaries downloaded into the artifact workspace and checked against SHA-256. Never send a font binary through a UTF-8 text resource.\\n4. Keep algorithms seeded where applicable, expose meaningful parameters and make every visible control work. For offline viewers, embed or locally serve p5.js and fonts rather than relying on a CDN at viewing time.\\n5. Render an actual PNG/PDF, inspect it at useful resolution for clipping, text, composition and artifacts, and retain editable drawing code, seed and brief. Test export and same-seed reproduction for interactive art.\\n6. Describe code illustrations as code illustrations. Do not imply they were produced by a diffusion model or are photographs.\\n\\n## Device and permission gates\\n\\nRequired authorization scopes: computer:read, computer:write. Execution uses existing device tools and current workspace permissions. Paid APIs, model weights and unrelated external side effects need their own authorization. This skill does not elevate Remote Arc Tool privileges or authorize hidden credentials.\\n\\n## Preflight\\n\\nA packaged Skill is guidance and resources, not proof of an installed engine. Check the chosen local renderer and fonts before relying on them. Avoid unnecessary model installation when code graphics satisfy the request.\\n\\n## Evidence of completion\\n\\nReport the actual file, dimensions, device and renderer; include editable source, seed when used and checks actually completed. Model-based work also identifies the backend and model. Distinguish rendering from visual verification and never imply execution succeeded from a plan alone.\\n\"},{\"id\":\"ui-inspector\",\"name\":\"UI Inspector\",\"priority\":\"P1\",\"category\":\"desktop\",\"description\":\"Understand screenshot controls with DOM first and optional OmniParser vision.\",\"engine\":\"OmniParser / screenshot tooling\",\"requires\":[\"python3\"],\"scopes\":[\"computer:read\"],\"upstream\":\"https://github.com/microsoft/OmniParser\",\"license\":\"Review repository and model terms\",\"optIn\":true,\"content\":\"---\\nname: ui-inspector\\ndescription: Understand screenshot controls with DOM first and optional OmniParser vision.\\npriority: P1\\ncategory: desktop\\nstatus: builtin-guidance\\n---\\n\\n# UI Inspector\\n\\n**Scope:** OmniParser / screenshot tooling. **Source:** https://github.com/microsoft/OmniParser\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\nFollow DOM/accessibility tree first for browser pages. For arbitrary desktop screenshots with permission, optionally use OmniParser to identify UI elements and candidate click targets. Treat visual coordinates as guesses: check display scaling, multiple screens, app focus, occlusion and the target before using existing mouse/process tools. Do not auto-install large vision model weights, silently send private screenshots to hosted APIs, or act based on a low-confidence bounding box. Use cropped screenshots only with explicit consent. Verify each mutation by a fresh screenshot or accessibility snapshot; stop after repeated misses.\\n\\n## Device and permission gates\\n\\nRequired authorization scopes: computer:read. Scripts, OS/desktop actions, external service side effects, media generation and paid API usage require independent user-approved execution permissions. This skill does not elevate Remote Arc Tool privileges or authorize use of hidden credentials.\\n\\n## Preflight\\n\\nCheck the engines this skill names before relying on them, with tools you already have (for example `<engine> --version` through start_process, or list_directory on its install folder). Missing executors must be installed explicitly on the correct user device. A Skill being built-in is NOT evidence an engine is present.\\n\\n## Evidence of completion\\n\\nReport input constraints, executed tools and device, verified output path/result, permissions used, cost (if known) and failures. Do not imply successful execution from merely writing a plan.\\n\"},{\"id\":\"transcribe-subtitles\",\"name\":\"Transcribe & Subtitles\",\"priority\":\"P1\",\"category\":\"media\",\"description\":\"Transcribe audio/video into finished SRT/VTT subtitles locally; automatically install whisper.cpp, FFmpeg and the base multilingual model when needed.\",\"engine\":\"whisper.cpp + FFmpeg\",\"requires\":[\"ffmpeg\"],\"scopes\":[\"computer:read\",\"computer:write\"],\"upstream\":\"https://github.com/ggml-org/whisper.cpp\",\"license\":\"MIT; model weights separate\",\"optIn\":false,\"content\":\"---\\nname: transcribe-subtitles\\ndescription: Produce finished SRT/VTT subtitles locally from audio or video; automatically prepare whisper.cpp, FFmpeg and the base multilingual model when needed.\\npriority: P1\\ncategory: media\\nstatus: builtin-guidance\\n---\\n\\n# Transcribe & Subtitles\\n\\nTranscribe on the user's own computer using whisper.cpp + FFmpeg. Keep recordings, extracted audio, inference and results on that device. Deliver actual .srt/.vtt files, and a subtitled video if requested.\\n\\n## Device and permission gates\\n\\nUse an online user device with read_file, write_file and start_process inside an authorized Trusted Write Location. A transcription task authorizes ordinary task-local dependency preparation within those permissions. Automatically download/install missing FFmpeg, whisper.cpp and the multilingual base model; announce the source, approximate size and destination, then continue. Do not stop to ask the user to install them manually. No system/admin installation, cloud transcription, paid API or input-media upload is authorized by this skill.\\n\\n## Prepare the local helper\\n\\nUse scripts/local-media.mjs packaged with this skill. Arc prepare_skill {id:\\\"transcribe-subtitles\\\",deviceId,workspace} stages it. Connect read_skill {id:\\\"transcribe-subtitles\\\",path:\\\"scripts/local-media.mjs\\\",offset:0} returns exact chunks; follow nextOffset to null, write them locally and compare SHA-256 with the manifest. Resource-only clients can read remotearc://skills/transcribe-subtitles/scripts/local-media.mjs.\\n\\nUse Node.js 22.2+ and npm. If missing, prepare a portable Node distribution from nodejs.org/dist inside .ra-media-tools/node and verify the published release SHASUMS256.txt; no administrator/global installation. Read video-creator for that preparation method if needed.\\n\\n    node \\\"<helper>/scripts/local-media.mjs\\\" ensure --workspace \\\"<workspace>\\\" --components whisper\\n\\nThe helper reuses working binaries, otherwise downloads checksum-verified pinned releases, or builds pinned whisper.cpp locally with a portable CMake. macOS/Linux require an existing C++ compiler and git; if an OS developer-tools dialog is necessary, report that specific blocker without bypassing OS consent. Windows x64 uses the CPU binary with its DLLs. GPU is optional; no CUDA/PyTorch download by default.\\n\\n**Default model:** `ggml-base.bin` (multilingual, ~148 MB / 141 MiB), pinned to a Hugging Face revision and SHA-256. Keep caches under <workspace>/.ra-media-tools. Initial setup may also need tens or hundreds of MB for FFmpeg/build tools. Check free disk first and reuse verified caches on later runs. ggml-small.bin (~466 MB) or ggml-large-v3-turbo.bin (~1.6 GB) require explicit size-aware consent before a larger download; do not silently substitute them after poor recognition. Qwen TTS and ACE-Step installation always follows their size-aware confirmation gate.\\n\\n## Produce and verify subtitles\\n\\n1. Inspect the input duration and audio track with ffprobe. For long recordings, disclose likely CPU runtime; run as a managed background process so it can finish and retain logs.\\n2. Execute:\\n       node \\\"<helper>/scripts/local-media.mjs\\\" transcribe --workspace \\\"<workspace>\\\" --input \\\"<audio or video>\\\" --output \\\"<new subtitle prefix>\\\" --language auto\\n   Use zh/en when the language is known. The helper prepares dependencies, extracts 16 kHz mono WAV, runs whisper-cli, reads generated SRT/VTT and validates nonempty timed cues, ordering and source-duration bounds. It refuses to overwrite existing output files and writes an artifact receipt. Silence/no speech is reported explicitly.\\n3. Read the output, review a sample against the original audio, correct observed mistakes and verify names/terminology. Never invent dialogue or speaker identities. Timed files alone do not establish recognition accuracy.\\n4. For burned-in subtitles, use local FFmpeg with the correct installed font (CJK for Chinese), fit subtitles inside safe margins, then ffprobe and decode the resulting MP4. Inspect frames and audio sync. For OpenFilm, put a matching .vtt beside its clip.\\n5. Deliver .srt and .vtt (and the MP4 if asked), absolute local paths, model name, covered duration and unverified portions. A temporary create_file_resource link is optional; keep the local durable copy and state link expiry.\\n\\n## Evidence of completion\\n\\nDo not claim subtitles exist before reading both files. Distinguish engine readiness, transcription completion, timing validation and human-language accuracy. Downloads contact official dependency/model hosts; user recordings never leave the selected device.\\n\"},{\"id\":\"research-reports\",\"name\":\"Research & Reports\",\"priority\":\"P0\",\"category\":\"productivity\",\"description\":\"Gather cited evidence from authorized sources and create reproducible reports.\",\"engine\":\"Browser Operator + existing file tools\",\"requires\":[],\"scopes\":[\"browser:read\",\"computer:read\",\"computer:write\"],\"upstream\":\"https://github.com/microsoft/playwright-mcp\",\"license\":\"RA authored skill instructions\",\"optIn\":false,\"content\":\"---\\nname: research-reports\\ndescription: Gather cited evidence from authorized sources and create reproducible reports.\\npriority: P0\\ncategory: productivity\\nstatus: builtin-guidance\\n---\\n\\n# Research & Reports\\n\\n**Scope:** Browser Operator + existing file tools. **Source:** https://github.com/microsoft/playwright-mcp\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\nDefine a precise research question, time window, and trustworthy sources. Read page text via existing authorized browser tools where possible; for bulk web automation opt-in to Browser Operator. Maintain source URLs and timestamps; separate fact from inference and avoid invented citations. Synthesize clear claims with a supporting evidence table, flag uncertainty and contradicting sources. Deliver Markdown, HTML or a requested artifact in a user-authorized workspace; verify paths, file content and citations. Never scrape sites in violation of user scope or access private pages without explicit authorization.\\n\\n## Device and permission gates\\n\\nRequired authorization scopes: browser:read, computer:read, computer:write. Scripts, OS/desktop actions, external service side effects, media generation and paid API usage require independent user-approved execution permissions. This skill does not elevate Remote Arc Tool privileges or authorize use of hidden credentials.\\n\\n## Preflight\\n\\nCheck the engines this skill names before relying on them, with tools you already have (for example `<engine> --version` through start_process, or list_directory on its install folder). Missing executors must be installed explicitly on the correct user device. A Skill being built-in is NOT evidence an engine is present.\\n\\n## Evidence of completion\\n\\nReport input constraints, executed tools and device, verified output path/result, permissions used, cost (if known) and failures. Do not imply successful execution from merely writing a plan.\\n\"},{\"id\":\"scheduled-routines\",\"name\":\"Scheduled Routines\",\"priority\":\"P0\",\"category\":\"productivity\",\"description\":\"Create repeatable, bounded, resumable tasks using the existing Remote Arc scheduler.\",\"engine\":\"Remote Arc automation runtime\",\"requires\":[],\"scopes\":[\"automation:read\",\"automation:write\"],\"upstream\":\"https://remotearc.app\",\"license\":\"RA authored skill instructions\",\"optIn\":false,\"content\":\"---\\nname: scheduled-routines\\ndescription: Create repeatable, bounded, resumable tasks using the existing Remote Arc scheduler.\\npriority: P0\\ncategory: productivity\\nstatus: builtin-guidance\\n---\\n\\n# Scheduled Routines\\n\\n**Scope:** Remote Arc automation runtime. **Source:** https://remotearc.app\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\nUse Remote Arc's existing automation/goal scheduler; this is a method, not a new MCP tool. Agree on trigger, goal, authorized device, permitted apps and paths, output artifact, spending/time budgets, review gates and stop condition. Prefer event-driven checks before calling a model; keep idempotent checkpoints, bounded retries and an audit trail. Pause and resume without losing task state. Explicit confirmation is required for outgoing emails, publishing, purchases and destructive actions. Validate actual outcome using source-of-truth data before marking complete. If the device is asleep/offline, park the run rather than claiming success. Never schedule unbounded paid API loops.\\n\\n## Device and permission gates\\n\\nRequired authorization scopes: automation:read, automation:write. Scripts, OS/desktop actions, external service side effects, media generation and paid API usage require independent user-approved execution permissions. This skill does not elevate Remote Arc Tool privileges or authorize use of hidden credentials.\\n\\n## Preflight\\n\\nCheck the engines this skill names before relying on them, with tools you already have (for example `<engine> --version` through start_process, or list_directory on its install folder). Missing executors must be installed explicitly on the correct user device. A Skill being built-in is NOT evidence an engine is present.\\n\\n## Evidence of completion\\n\\nReport input constraints, executed tools and device, verified output path/result, permissions used, cost (if known) and failures. Do not imply successful execution from merely writing a plan.\\n\"},{\"id\":\"remotion-video\",\"name\":\"Remotion Video\",\"priority\":\"P0\",\"category\":\"media\",\"description\":\"Core local video skill: React scenes, Three.js cameras, particles, data animation and beat-aligned editing with Remotion.\",\"engine\":\"Remotion 4.0.538 / React / Three.js + FFmpeg\",\"requires\":[\"node\",\"npm\",\"ffmpeg\"],\"scopes\":[\"computer:read\",\"computer:write\"],\"upstream\":\"https://github.com/remotion-dev/remotion\",\"license\":\"RA-authored workflow; Remotion software license applies\",\"optIn\":false,\"content\":\"---\\nname: remotion-video\\ndescription: RA core local filmmaking with Remotion, React and optional Three.js. Use for programmable scenes, cinematic camera work, explainers, data animation and music-timed edits.\\npriority: P0\\ncategory: media\\nstatus: builtin-guidance\\n---\\n\\n# Remotion Video\\n\\nUse Remotion as a core RA video option when React components, reusable scenes, synchronized data, three-dimensional camera work or precise frame control fit the brief. HyperFrames is another option for HTML/SVG/GSAP motion; OpenFilm fits shot and clip assembly. Match the user's chosen engine and outcome.\\n\\n## Device and permission gates\\n\\nRender on an authorized user computer, inside its Trusted Write Locations. The media request authorizes ordinary task-local Node/npm, Chromium, FFmpeg and Remotion dependencies under the existing device permissions. Disclose their source, destination and approximate download size, then prepare them as needed without a second installation question. Do not upload footage or render in the cloud unless requested.\\n\\nQwen TTS and ACE-Step need explicit size-aware approval before downloading their multi-GB runtimes or weights. Reuse an already installed compatible model or a prior approval covering the same installation. Narration or music requests alone do not authorize those downloads. Read video-creator for local audio alternatives and the consent boundary.\\n\\nRemotion has its own software license: https://www.remotion.dev/license. Follow the terms applicable to the user's use; do not describe it as universally free or buy a license on the user's behalf. RA's workflow is original guidance and does not redistribute the separate upstream agent-skills repository.\\n\\n## Prepare and render\\n\\nThe package includes scripts/local-media.mjs, identical to the helper used by video-creator. In Arc use prepare_skill with this skill ID, deviceId and workspace. In Connect read_skill lists hashed resources; retrieve the helper by path/offset through the final chunk and verify its SHA-256 before writing it locally.\\n\\n    node \\\"<skill>/scripts/local-media.mjs\\\" ensure --workspace \\\"<workspace>\\\" --components remotion\\n\\nThis prepares pinned Remotion 4.0.538, React, Three.js/React Three Fiber, local fonts support, Chromium and FFmpeg when needed. Initial downloads can be hundreds of MB. Keep project package versions aligned with the pinned helper and use the returned module directory, browser path and CLI rather than global npx/latest. Dependency readiness is not a finished video.\\n\\nCreate a separate project with a registered Composition and local public assets. For the reusable cache's npm dependencies, link or resolve project modules to the returned engine directory within the authorized workspace, or install the same pinned dependencies in the project. Preserve existing project files and lockfiles.\\n\\n    node \\\"<skill>/scripts/local-media.mjs\\\" render --workspace \\\"<workspace>\\\" --engine remotion --project \\\"<project>\\\" --entry src/index.tsx --composition Main --output \\\"<new film>.mp4\\\"\\n\\nThe helper passes the verified local browser path and a bounded concurrency, renders a real MP4, checks streams/duration, decodes it and saves an artifact receipt. Run long jobs in the device process manager and read their completion status and logs. Never equate starting a render with success.\\n\\n## Authoring decisions that matter\\n\\n- Derive visual state from useCurrentFrame and useVideoConfig. Do not use wall clocks, unseeded randomness or independent CSS animation for rendered motion.\\n- Use separate scene components and purposeful cuts, camera changes or transformations. When comparing engines, design different visual treatments rather than relabeling identical frames.\\n- For Three.js use ThreeCanvas from @remotion/three, frame-driven transforms, actual lighting and locally generated or authorized textures. Wait for fonts before screen textures are drawn; release the font hold only after the canvas has mounted. preserveDrawingBuffer:true may be needed for reliable WebGL capture. Check actual rendered stills: a successful render can still contain a blank canvas. Prefer deterministic sprite/particle halos if a postprocessing chain does not advance reliably.\\n- For requested beat edits, analyze the chosen local music, align scene/action anchors to detected transients at the chosen frame rate, and keep enough time for subtitles. Retiming the visuals must also update the subtitle file and chapter markers. Inspect actual output cuts with audio, not just a timeline diagram.\\n- User-specified language, duration, narration and music take priority. Keep the recording's original attribution; do not claim that supplied music is original or royalty-free.\\n\\n## Verify and deliver\\n\\nInspect rendered keyframes and transitions, especially small text, the longest caption, first/last frames, and three-dimensional canvas content. Check actual duration, resolution, frame count, audio coverage and full decode; review speech/subtitle alignment where applicable. Keep numeric event counters consistent with the explained events instead of tweening them into fictitious transactions.\\n\\nDeliver the playable file and its absolute local path, with editable sources and checks that actually ran. A temporary Remote Arc file resource is an expiring download, not permanent hosting. Publish only to a destination the user requested.\\n\"},{\"id\":\"hyperframes-video\",\"name\":\"HyperFrames Video\",\"priority\":\"P0\",\"category\":\"media\",\"description\":\"Local HTML/SVG/Canvas and GSAP videos for interface demos, motion typography, diagrams and layered explainers.\",\"engine\":\"HyperFrames 0.8.193 / GSAP + FFmpeg\",\"requires\":[\"node\",\"npm\",\"ffmpeg\"],\"scopes\":[\"computer:read\",\"computer:write\"],\"upstream\":\"https://github.com/heygen-com/hyperframes\",\"license\":\"RA-authored workflow; upstream engine terms apply\",\"optIn\":false,\"content\":\"---\\nname: hyperframes-video\\ndescription: Create local HTML, SVG, Canvas and GSAP videos with HyperFrames. Use for interface demos, motion typography, animated diagrams and layered explainers.\\npriority: P0\\ncategory: media\\nstatus: builtin-guidance\\n---\\n\\n# HyperFrames Video\\n\\nChoose HyperFrames for browser-native motion: typography, glass interfaces, SVG paths, diagrams, dashboards and timed overlays. Use Remotion when the brief benefits from React scene composition or an established Three.js camera workflow; follow an explicitly requested engine.\\n\\n## Device and permission gates\\n\\nUse an authorized user device and writable task workspace. Read video-creator for the shared local-rendering workflow, source handling and verification. Ordinary HyperFrames, browser and FFmpeg dependencies may be prepared automatically within that media request and current permissions, after disclosing download source, approximate size and destination. All rendering stays local. Qwen TTS and ACE-Step downloads still require size-aware installation consent.\\n\\n## Execute\\n\\nThis package includes the same hashed scripts/local-media.mjs as video-creator. Materialize it with prepare_skill in Arc or chunked read_skill resource reads in Connect.\\n\\n    node \\\"<skill>/scripts/local-media.mjs\\\" ensure --workspace \\\"<workspace>\\\" --components hyperframes\\n    node \\\"<skill>/scripts/local-media.mjs\\\" render --workspace \\\"<workspace>\\\" --engine hyperframes --project \\\"<project>\\\" --output \\\"<new film>.mp4\\\"\\n\\nUse the installed pinned CLI's documentation. In index.html provide composition dimensions, data-fps, data-duration and a window.__timelines entry. Register paused, seekable GSAP timelines. Give every timed media element a stable id, local src and correct duration. Bundle fonts and scripts locally. Canvas particles must be deterministic functions of the seek time; do not use wall-clock loops or fresh randomness.\\n\\nAnimate the explanation itself: let a path carry an order, make a queue react to a sell order, or transform data into a new view. Use music-derived edit points when requested, preserve reading time, and update subtitle timing when retiming scenes. Test forward and backward seeks before full rendering.\\n\\n## Verify and deliver\\n\\nResolve lint errors, including missing media IDs and unregistered timelines. Check actual captured frames, scene boundaries, text layout, particles, audio throughout the film and the final frame. Verify the finished MP4 with ffprobe and full decode. Deliver that file and editable HTML/JS sources; a preparation receipt alone is not a video.\\n\"},{\"id\":\"openfilm-video\",\"name\":\"OpenFilm Video\",\"priority\":\"P0\",\"category\":\"media\",\"description\":\"Local shot-based video assembly with original code graphics, owned clips, transitions and timed audio.\",\"engine\":\"OpenFilm 0.2.5 + FFmpeg\",\"requires\":[\"node\",\"npm\",\"ffmpeg\"],\"scopes\":[\"computer:read\",\"computer:write\"],\"upstream\":\"https://github.com/openfilm/openfilm\",\"license\":\"MIT; RA-authored workflow\",\"optIn\":false,\"content\":\"---\\nname: openfilm-video\\ndescription: Assemble and render local code-driven shots and media tracks with OpenFilm. Use for clip-based editing, transitions, product sequences and reusable shot pages.\\npriority: P0\\ncategory: media\\nstatus: builtin-guidance\\n---\\n\\n# OpenFilm Video\\n\\nUse OpenFilm for a shot-based timeline combining owned footage, local audio and code-driven pages. For React/Three.js scene composition use remotion-video; for HTML/SVG/GSAP motion use hyperframes-video when that better fits the task.\\n\\n## Device and permission gates\\n\\nRender on an authorized user computer in a task workspace. Follow video-creator for source handling, ordinary automatic dependency preparation and finished-file checks. Announce download source, size and destination. Do not request another permission merely because ordinary OpenFilm, Chromium or FFmpeg is missing. Qwen TTS and ACE-Step installation retains its explicit large-download approval gate. Publishing or paid APIs require authorization covering that action.\\n\\n## Execute and verify\\n\\nMaterialize the packaged scripts/local-media.mjs through Arc prepare_skill or Connect's chunked, hashed resources.\\n\\n    node \\\"<skill>/scripts/local-media.mjs\\\" ensure --workspace \\\"<workspace>\\\" --components openfilm\\n    node \\\"<skill>/scripts/local-media.mjs\\\" render --workspace \\\"<workspace>\\\" --engine openfilm --project \\\"<project>\\\" --output \\\"<new film>.mp4\\\"\\n\\nRead the installed CLI manual before authoring. Give each code page a deterministic window.film duration/frame(t) interface. Compose tracks in film.html, keep audio on the composition timeline, and load scripts, fonts and images from local assets. Preserve OPENFILM_NO_BROWSER=1 and OPENFILM_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1 for unattended rendering; do not open a visible progress browser without a user need.\\n\\nUse meaningful scene actions and transitions; avoid filling the duration with title cards. If music timing is requested, align significant edits to the recording and retain readable captions. Verify contact sheets, transition frames, actual stream duration, audio coverage and complete decoding. Deliver the MP4 and editable shot sources.\\n\"},{\"id\":\"map-creator\",\"name\":\"Map Creator\",\"priority\":\"P1\",\"category\":\"media\",\"description\":\"Create real SVG maps and animated geographic scenes locally with D3 and attributed Natural Earth geometry.\",\"engine\":\"D3 / Natural Earth + local video engines\",\"requires\":[\"node\",\"npm\"],\"scopes\":[\"computer:read\",\"computer:write\"],\"upstream\":\"https://www.naturalearthdata.com/\",\"license\":\"RA-authored workflow; Natural Earth public-domain data\",\"optIn\":false,\"content\":\"---\\nname: map-creator\\ndescription: Draw and animate maps locally with Natural Earth and D3. Use for geographic explainers, highlighted countries, routes and map footage for videos.\\npriority: P1\\ncategory: media\\nstatus: builtin-guidance\\n---\\n\\n# Map Creator\\n\\nCreate an actual map file or animated map scene from attributed geometry and source data. Natural Earth and D3 cover many country-level explainers; choose an appropriate projection and fetch more detailed authorized data only when needed.\\n\\n## Device and permission gates\\n\\nUse an authorized local computer and workspace. Preparing ordinary D3 packages and public geometry is part of the requested map task: disclose source, size and destination, then continue within existing permissions. Rendering and private input data stay on the user's device. Do not invent administrative detail or imply that an illustrative map is a legal boundary or navigation product.\\n\\n## Execute and verify\\n\\nMaterialize this package's hashed scripts/local-media.mjs using prepare_skill or chunked read_skill resource reads.\\n\\n    node \\\"<skill>/scripts/local-media.mjs\\\" map --workspace \\\"<workspace>\\\" --output \\\"<new map>.svg\\\" --highlight NZL,CHN\\n\\nThe helper prepares pinned D3 packages, downloads country geometry, records its source revision/hash and produces a real SVG with an artifact receipt. For motion, copy local geometry into a Remotion, HyperFrames or OpenFilm project and animate projected geometry, labels or routes from the frame/seek time. Do not depend on a live tile CDN during rendering.\\n\\nVerify labels, route endpoints, projection clipping, color legend and highlighted country identifiers. Inspect the saved image or video and keep its source attribution. Deliver the file and reusable geometry/project when useful.\\n\"}]");
